```json
{"flags":[{"id":"dot-grids-for-every-count","tags":["unbuildable"],"problems":["motion.reduced: 'All dots appear filled at once' shows the wrong count under reduced motion, so the empty dots for what is left are lost.","motion.reduced hides the exact state the card exists to show."],"fix":"Make reduced motion show the final state at once: filled dots for the count, empty holes for the rest, with no stagger. Also add region \"global\" to match the other constraint cards."},{"id":"dots-carry-every-mark","tags":["vague","incoherent"],"problems":["texture: 'a single cluster of printed dots at 20 percent' does not say the dot size, the pattern or where on the card it sits.","palette: 'rice paper' and 'seal red' borrow an East Asian seal and paper mood that has nothing to do with a dot rule shared by Arabic and Latin.","imagery: punch cards have rectangular holes, not round dots, so they do not support the one-dot idea."],"fix":"Pick a palette that comes from ink dots on print (for example newsprint, press black and one stamp-pad accent) and drop the seal and rice paper names. Give the texture as an exact technique, such as a 4 px dot row at 20 percent along the top edge of the progress card, and swap punch cards for 'ink dot macro' or 'braille page'."},{"id":"every-heading-in-italics","tags":["fact"],"problems":["avoid: 'Arabic has no italics' is not true as stated, because slanted Arabic styles exist and the Amiri family on Google Fonts ships italic styles.","avoid: 'Amiri's display weight' does not exist; Amiri comes in regular and bold, not a separate display weight.","avoid: this item is a type instruction, not a way the card goes wrong."],"fix":"Move the Arabic rule into type: Arabic headings use Amiri Bold, set upright, and never use a CSS fake slant. Replace the avoid item with a real trap, such as obliqued Arabic made with a CSS transform."},{"id":"every-other-section-inverted","tags":["unbuildable","incoherent"],"problems":["imagery: showing photos as film negatives on the dark sections makes faces and products unreadable, which costs real content for a gimmick.","idea: a negative strip has no alternating light and dark pages, so the metaphor does not fit the rule.","motion: 'flips' at 200 ms does not say what flips or when it fires during scroll."],"fix":"Keep the photos positive on both grounds and set them in black and white with a tonal shift for the dark sections. Ground the idea in a newspaper broadsheet or a printed zine spread, and make the motion an exact 200 ms ground swap as the section edge crosses the top of the viewport."},{"id":"every-word-hand-lettered","tags":["incoherent","unbuildable"],"problems":["idea: it names a shop owner's chalk menu, but the palette is cream sketchbook paper with brush ink and the texture is dry brush, so it shows two different things.","surprise: an 'uneven baseline' on every label needs per-word custom work that the Caveat font alone does not give.","type: hand lettering on every button and label puts a script face on small UI controls, which hurts reading at those sizes."],"fix":"Pick one source: either a dark chalkboard surface with chalk white ink, or a marker-on-paper sign. Limit the lettering to headings and the main call to action, and keep small labels and buttons in the quiet text face."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
  {
    "id": "a-single-scrolling-ticker",
    "tags": ["unbuildable"],
    "problems": [
      "motion: 'scrolls' at 400 ms describes a band that moves forever. 400 ms says nothing about crawl speed, so a developer cannot build it as written.",
      "motion: no visible pause control is given for default users. Auto-moving content that runs longer than 5 s needs one, and pause on hover or focus does not cover touch users.",
      "motion: never stopping is the exact UI rule break the brief warns against, and the card treats it as normal behavior instead of bounding it."
    ],
    "fix": "Define the crawl as a speed (for example 60 px per second) that holds still for 2 s on each item and stops after one full cycle. Add a visible pause button on the band, and keep the 400 ms value only for the item-to-item step."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-and-white-only",
    "tags": ["incoherent"],
    "problems": [
      "palette: the idea says pure black and white with no grays, but the palette pads to four entries with #0a0a0a, #000000 and #fafafa. These are near duplicates that quietly bring in off values.",
      "palette: the accent '#000000 solid fill black' cannot be told apart from the ink '#0a0a0a carbon black', so no color actually leads."
    ],
    "fix": "Ship the palette honestly as #ffffff surface and #000000 ink only, and name inversion (a solid black fill) as the accent behavior in the idea. Drop the near-white and near-black fillers."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-white-and-one-gray",
    "tags": ["incoherent"],
    "problems": [
      "palette: the rule is three values, but the card lists four. Bone white #f5f5f3 and pure white #ffffff are two whites, which breaks the card's own 'no second gray' logic.",
      "texture: a 10 percent halftone dot over the concrete gray band produces an in-between tone, which adds a fourth value on the one surface meant to prove the rule."
    ],
    "fix": "Keep exactly #f5f5f3, #0d0d0d and #8c8c8c, and remove the pure white. Make the header band a flat concrete gray, or a halftone of pure black dots on white whose average lands on the one gray."
  },
  {
    "id": "ascii-characters-only",
    "tags": ["incoherent"],
    "problems": [
      "type: the card assigns Arabic fonts (Cascadia Mono, IBM Plex Sans Arabic), but Arabic letters are not ASCII. The rule as stated is broken the moment the app runs in Arabic, and the card never says how.",
      "avoid: nothing covers RTL. ASCII box art like +--+ and # bar charts needs a stated direction when the text inside runs right to left."
    ],
    "fix": "Restate the rule as 'all structure (borders, icons, charts, dividers) is ASCII; text runs in its own script'. Add one avoid line on keeping bar charts and box joins correct when the layout mirrors for RTL."
  }
]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "rajasthani-mirror-work", "tags": ["culture-name", "credit", "unbuildable"], "problems": ["name: 'Mirror Work' is generic and drops the region the id and notes depend on, and the card ignores the same craft in Sindh, Pakistan.", "culturalNotes: Credit says 'women from pastoral and craft communities' but names no actual community.", "texture: a silver radial gradient on primary buttons leaves the chalk cotton label on pale silver, which will fail contrast."], "fix": "Name it Rajasthani and Gujarati shisha embroidery. Credit named stitching communities only where you are sure, for example Rabari women, and note that the craft also lives in Sindh. Keep the silver disc as a small icon or a status dot beside the button label, with dark ink on any silver surface."}, {"id": "scandinavian-rosemaling", "tags": ["culture-name"], "problems": ["name: 'Scandinavian Rosemaling' blurs the tradition, while the card's own culturalNotes correctly call it Norwegian.", "nameAr: 'رسم الورود الإسكندنافي' repeats the loose regional label."], "fix": "Rename it 'Norwegian Rosemaling' with nameAr 'رسم الورود النرويجي'. Keep the Telemark and Hallingdal credit as written."}, {"id": "square-kufic-lettering", "tags": ["region", "sacred", "credit"], "problems": ["region: marked gulf, but square Kufic grew mainly in Iranian and Central Asian banna'i brickwork, not as a Gulf form.", "artQueries: 'kufic minaret inscription' points references straight at mosque inscriptions, which are mostly sacred text.", "culturalNotes: the credit to makers 'across the Islamic world' is loose. It should name where banna'i square Kufic was built."], "fix": "Set region to mena. Replace the minaret query with 'banna'i brick pattern' or 'square kufic grid'. Credit the brick and tile makers of Iran and Central Asia who built banna'i square Kufic, alongside the wider Arabic calligraphic tradition."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
  {"id": "op-art-1965", "tags": ["unbuildable", "incoherent"], "problems": ["texture: puts a warped 4 px stripe field directly behind the hero number, which makes the most important figure vibrate and hard to read.", "avoid: says 'Stripe patterns behind text or form fields', which contradicts the card's own texture."], "fix": "Keep the warped stripe field in a separate header band or card beside the hero number, and set the number on a plain gallery white or solid black block so it reads cleanly."},
  {"id": "psychedelic-1967", "tags": ["vague", "unbuildable"], "problems": ["texture: 'a slow oil-and-water blob gradient' does not say whether it moves, how fast, or whether it ever stops, so it may become an endless animation behind the headline.", "texture: no colors, blob size or opacity are given, so two designers would build different gradients, and a moving wash behind the liquid headline will hurt reading."], "fix": "Make the texture a static two-color blob gradient (purple to magenta, for example) at a set opacity inside the header band, or limit it to one 480 ms morph on load that settles and is replaced by a still frame under reduced motion."},
  {"id": "new-wave-1983", "tags": ["weak-copy"], "problems": ["surprise: spends the sentence comparing itself to Memphis instead of saying why the stepped layout is unexpected in UI and what it gives the interface.", "artQueries: 'new wave typography poster' and 'layered collage poster' are style phrases, not concrete nouns for the real thing."], "fix": "Rewrite the surprise around the mechanic itself, for example: diagonal type steps and overlapping planes let the interface show reading order without boxes. Replace the queries with concrete nouns such as 'letraset sheet' and 'basel school poster'."}
]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "faux-fur", "tags": ["unbuildable"], "problems": ["idea: puts the fur pile on the main button, which carries a label, but the card's own avoid list bans fur behind text.", "texture: 'strand-noise fur rendered as an SVG or shader edge' is heavy custom rendering, and the card gives no strand length, density or fallback, so two developers would ship very different things.", "motion: 'ruffles' does not say what moves, how far, or whether it runs on hover, press or idle."], "fix": "Put the fur only on a label-free shape, such as the avatar ring or a sticker badge, built as a pre-rendered fur PNG or SVG edge with a flat fallback. Make the motion a 320 ms ease-out press where the pile edge compresses 2 px, then settles."}, {"id": "debossed-leather", "tags": ["incoherent"], "problems": ["family: 'soft, rubber or inflatable' does not fit tanned saddle leather, which is a hide and not rubber or an inflatable.", "The wrong family will group this card with balloons and rubber stamps when the tool bends a page, which breaks the leather system."], "fix": "Move the card to 'textile and fiber', the closest family for hide goods, and keep the rest of the system as written."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
  {
    "id": "photos-only-no-shapes",
    "tags": ["unbuildable"],
    "problems": [
      "idea: 'buttons, dividers and backgrounds are all cropped pictures edge to edge' puts every label and body line over a photo, which is the text over busy art trap the avoid list itself names.",
      "idea: photo crops used as buttons give no clear affordance, and nothing says how hover, disabled or pressed states show, so state is hidden.",
      "texture and idea: full bleed photo backgrounds on every surface break the rule that texture and imagery stay on one surface."
    ],
    "fix": "Keep body text and controls on the darkroom black surface. Let photos carry navigation as contact sheet frames: tap a frame to select it, and mark the selection with the grease pencil red circle plus a text label. Drop photo backgrounds behind text entirely."
  },
  {
    "id": "split-screen-down-the-middle",
    "tags": ["unbuildable"],
    "problems": [
      "texture: filling the whole right half of the hero with deep ledger blue is a background wash on half the screen, not a texture on one surface.",
      "palette: press black ink on deep ledger blue (#1f3a5f) fails 4.5:1, and the card never says which ink the blue half uses."
    ],
    "fix": "Keep both halves on left page cream and mark the seam with a 2 percent paper grain strip only along the fold. If the blue half stays, set its text in left page cream and say so in the texture field."
  },
  {
    "id": "outlines-only-no-fills",
    "tags": ["generic"],
    "problems": [
      "palette: dark sheet, chalk line ink and one bright accent nearly repeat outlined-type-never-filled (blueprint night, chalk line white, highlighter lime).",
      "motion and texture: the same verb 'traces' and the same dot grid on the hero repeat that card, so the two cards read as one idea.",
      "surprise: says state is shown 'by stroke weight and color' but never gives the steps, for example 1.5 px rest, 3 px active, mint on focus."
    ],
    "fix": "Set it apart from the outlined type card. Use a light drafting vellum surface and a single stroke scale tied to state (1 px idle, 2 px hover, 3 px mint active), and swap the dot grid for a 1 px dimension line callout on the hero card only."
  },
  {
    "id": "serif-type-only",
    "tags": ["weak-copy"],
    "problems": [
      "nameAr: 'خطوط بسنات فقط' is not a natural Arabic or Saudi term for serif type and will read as a mistake to Arabic reviewers.",
      "type: Arabic has no serifs, and the card never says what the rule means for the Arabic side, beyond picking Amiri and Noto Naskh Arabic."
    ],
    "fix": "Use a clear name such as 'خطوط مذيلة فقط' or 'خط النسخ فقط'. Add one sentence saying that on the Arabic side the rule maps to Naskh with stroke contrast, including button labels, and that Kufi and geometric sans are never used."
  }
]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "hong-kong-minibus-sign", "tags": ["credit", "incoherent"], "problems": ["culturalNotes: 'Credit: Hong Kong sign makers and minibus drivers' is loose. It does not name the craft: the hand-lettered placards painted for the public light buses.", "texture: 'slight print misregistration' belongs to printed matter, not to hand-lettered placards, so it contradicts the card's own idea.", "type: Archivo Narrow as display gives a clean grotesk. Nothing in the type carries the hand-lettered stroke the idea promises."], "fix": "Credit the hand-lettering placard makers of Hong Kong's public light buses as a named craft, without inventing any individual makers. Replace misregistration with a visible brush-stroke edge on the price numerals of the one placard card, and choose a display face whose weight and stroke echo the painted numerals."}, {"id": "karak-tea-glass", "tags": ["incoherent"], "problems": ["type: the voice is 'rounded', but the display is Bricolage Grotesque, a grotesk, and the Arabic display Alexandria is geometric. The voice label and the fonts disagree."], "fix": "Pick one direction and make all the type fields match it. Either set the voice to grotesk and keep Bricolage Grotesque with a Kufi or geometric Arabic display, or keep 'rounded' and switch to a truly rounded Latin display with a rounded Arabic display such as Baloo Bhaijaan 2."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "clock-faces", "tags": ["unbuildable", "incoherent"], "problems": ["motion: a red second hand that ticks every second is motion that never stops, which breaks the brief's UI rule and goes against the card's own avoid item about a hand that pulls attention.", "idea: a clean railway dial with a red second hand as the one accent points straight at the Swiss railway clock, a protected and licensed design, while avoid says not to copy a real watch brand's dial.", "texture: minute ticks plus a 3 percent glass sheen stacks two treatments on the hero dial, and 'soft glass sheen' does not say how to build it."], "fix": "Move the hands only on a real state change (a timer starting, a step finishing) at 120 to 300 ms. Under reduced motion, show the final position with no motion. Describe a generic station dial without the red lollipop second hand, and drop the glass sheen so the tick ring is the only texture."}, {"id": "fingerprints", "tags": ["unbuildable"], "problems": ["surprise: a 'confirm-by-pressing' fingerprint mechanic looks like biometric authentication when nothing is being checked, which misleads users about security.", "imagery: 'real ridge patterns' conflicts with the avoid item 'copying a real person's print', and real prints are biometric data."], "fix": "Make the pressed print a decorative stamp on a receipt or profile card, never a confirm control that looks like a biometric check. Draw the loop, whorl and arch as synthetic vector ridges, not crops of real prints."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "everything-ticks-once-per-second", "tags": ["unbuildable"], "problems": ["idea: snapping all motion to a shared one-second beat means a tap or toggle can wait up to 1000 ms for visual feedback, which hides state and makes the UI feel broken.", "motion: a tick on every second is motion that never stops, and nothing on the card limits it to a time-bound element.", "motion.reduced: still updates on the beat, so the input delay remains for users who asked for less motion."], "fix": "Limit the beat to live data and the hero clock number only, and let direct feedback (press, toggle, focus) respond at once in 120 to 160 ms. Under reduced motion, stop the ring ticks and update values in place with an instant swap."}, {"id": "line-height-at-1-8", "tags": ["vague", "generic"], "problems": ["motion: 'breathes' at 400 ms does not say what moves, and the reduced note implies line spacing animates, which reflows text while it is being read.", "imagery: photos of lined notebooks and ledgers illustrate the rule instead of giving a product a picture style, so they would not fit most screens.", "palette: pale sage, pine and clay orange have nothing to do with leading; with a new name the card would read the same."], "fix": "Tie the system to set reading matter: a book or ruled exercise page palette, a texture placed under one reading card, and a real verb such as a 200 ms fade-in of text blocks with the line height fixed. Make imagery a concrete crop rule (for example images whose height is a whole multiple of the 1.8 line)."}, {"id": "lowercase-letters-only", "tags": ["generic", "weak-copy"], "problems": ["palette, imagery: oat milk, persimmon and 'warm natural-light photos of hands and objects' is a default lifestyle look with no link to lowercase type.", "motion: 'settles' is not drawn from anything about lowercase letters.", "surprise: 'friendly and modern calm' is filler; the real source named in artQueries (Bauhaus lowercase typography) is never used in the system."], "fix": "Build from the Bauhaus single-alphabet tradition the artQueries point to: a printed primary palette with a black ink, one strong geometric lowercase display moment, and a set-in-metal or printed motion verb. Make imagery a concrete treatment that matches it, such as flat geometric illustration or hard-cropped black and white photos."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
  {"id": "girih-tiles", "tags": ["culture-name", "credit", "region", "vague"], "problems": ["culturalNotes: 'Islamic architecture across Persia, Central Asia and Anatolia' names the tradition loosely by religion and spreads it over three regions as if they were one.", "culturalNotes: the Credit line, 'tile makers and geometers of the Persian and wider Islamic architectural tradition', names no specific people, period or place.", "texture: '12 percent turquoise line weight' does not say a stroke width, so two designers would draw different lines.", "nameAr: 'زخرفة الكره' is ambiguous in Arabic, because كره reads as ball or hatred rather than the knot pattern."], "fix": "Anchor the card in one place and period, for example girih tilework of Timurid and Safavid Iran made by Persian tile cutters and master builders, and credit them by name of craft and region. Give the strapwork an exact width (1.5 px turquoise line at 60 percent on the hero card), and use a clear Arabic name or a transliteration with a gloss."},
  {"id": "hawaiian-aloha-prints", "tags": ["credit", "fact"], "problems": ["culturalNotes: credits the Native Hawaiian kapa tradition for shaping the aloha print, a link the card cannot stand behind.", "culturalNotes: leaves out the Japanese and Chinese immigrant tailors and makers in Honolulu who are generally linked to the early aloha shirt."], "fix": "Credit Honolulu's garment makers, including the Japanese and Chinese immigrant tailors and the local print designers, and drop the kapa claim. Keep Native Hawaiian symbols out, as the card already says."},
  {"id": "ikat-weaving", "tags": ["region", "credit", "culture-name"], "problems": ["region: 'global' with weavers from Uzbekistan, Indonesia, India and Guatemala mixes unrelated traditions as if they were one style.", "culturalNotes: the Credit line, 'name the specific ikat tradition you draw from', hands the credit to the reader and names no people.", "palette: marigold, cochineal and teal are not tied to any one ikat tradition, so the color story has no home."], "fix": "Pick one tradition, for example Uzbek abrbandi silk ikat of the Fergana Valley and Bukhara, and credit its weavers and dyers. Set the region to that place (central-asia) and take the palette from its actual dye range."}
]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "leaves", "tags": ["generic", "weak-copy"], "problems": ["idea: this is the same herbarium-sheet system as the flowers card: pressed specimen, typed label, tape strip, cream paper.", "surprise: repeats the flowers card's specimen-and-label structure almost word for word, so the word adds nothing of its own.", "palette: cream, forest green and fig leaf green with dried sage reads as generic eco branding, which is the trap the avoid list warns about."], "fix": "Give leaves a mechanic of its own that the flowers card does not have, such as vein structure as a branching tree navigation or leaf rubbings in graphite on one card. Otherwise drop it as a near duplicate of flowers."}, {"id": "kilometer-stones", "tags": ["region"], "problems": ["region: the card is marked global, but the red-capped limestone distance stone it describes belongs to particular European national road systems, not to every country.", "imagery: 'weathered roadside stones' with a red cap band depends on that one national design, so the card is not actually placeless."], "fix": "Mark the region as europe and name the form as the red-capped kilometre marker of French and other European national roads. Or drop the red cap and draw on a plain, generic carved distance stone so global stays true."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [{"id": "soap-bubble-film", "tags": ["unbuildable"], "problems": ["texture: a 'slow-swirling' gradient on every round chip and avatar is a loop that never stops, running across many elements at once.", "motion.reduced: covers only the pop and says nothing about stopping the swirl under prefers-reduced-motion."], "fix": "Make the film a static thin-film gradient ring that shifts hue once, in about 400 ms, on hover or focus of the chip, with no idle loop. Under reduced motion the ring stays static."}, {"id": "spun-sugar", "tags": ["incoherent"], "problems": ["family: 'glass and clear plastic' is wrong for spun sugar, which is a candy material and belongs in 'liquid, wax and candy'.", "palette: the surface name 'cream' is a placeholder, not a real name tied to the word."], "fix": "Move the card to 'liquid, wax and candy' and rename the surface after something from sugar work, such as 'fondant cream' or 'pastry plate white'."}, {"id": "stained-glass", "tags": ["credit", "culture-name"], "problems": ["culturalNotes Credit: 'the stained glass craft and the glaziers' names no region or people, so it is a generic credit.", "culturalNotes Meaning: frames the craft as only European, while nameAr 'زجاج معشق' is the Arabic name for the gypsum-and-glass window craft of Yemen and the Levant, so the card blurs two traditions."], "fix": "Pick one tradition. Either credit European leaded glass and the glaziers of medieval and later European workshops and use an Arabic name for that, or rebuild the card as qamariya windows of Yemen and give it its own credit and region."}, {"id": "sea-urchin-shell", "tags": ["weak-copy", "vague"], "problems": ["surprise: repeats the idea (five-fold dot rows make a radial grid) and adds no new mechanic or feeling.", "motion: 'dots in' is not a physical verb, and it does not say the order, direction or easing of the dots.", "avoid: 'Pastel concept comps' is generic filler, not a risk specific to this word."], "fix": "Rewrite surprise around what the five-fold symmetry gives the UI, for example five-step ratings that fill one ambulacrum row at a time. Make the motion a concrete verb such as 'beads outward', with dots filling from the center pole in 40 ms steps."}]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
  {"id": "tokyo-1964-olympics", "tags": ["flag-emblem", "incoherent"], "problems": ["idea: a single solid red circle on wide white space is the Hinomaru, the national flag of Japan, so the card's main move is the flag used as decoration.", "avoid: it bans a centered red circle on a plain white rectangle, but the idea itself sets one red circle on white space, so the rule contradicts the card.", "imagery: athletes 'cropped tight against a dark ground' clash with the airy rice paper white system the idea describes.", "texture: 15 percent offset grain is heavy for a card built on calm white space."], "fix": "Make the lead shape something from the 1964 grammar that is not the flag: pictogram-style icons on a strict grid, or an off-center red arc or band beside gold, so no screen reads as the Hinomaru. Put the photos on the white ground, and drop the grain to 3 percent on the hero card only."},
  {"id": "web-brutalism-2015", "tags": ["incoherent"], "problems": ["idea: it calls for 'browser defaults' but sets 'black monospace text', while the browser default is a serif (Times New Roman), and the artQueries even search for 'Times New Roman webpage'.", "palette: selection yellow is named as a default, but browsers select text in blue by default, so the 'raw defaults' rule breaks in its own palette."], "fix": "Pick one direction. Either commit to true defaults (a Times-like serif such as Libre Caslon Text or Newsreader for body, and Space Mono only for code and captions), or reframe the idea as 'mono brutalist sites' and drop the defaults claim. Rename selection yellow as a chosen highlight, not a default."},
  {"id": "alula-sandstone-strata", "tags": ["credit", "vague"], "problems": ["culturalNotes: the Credit line credits 'the landscape of AlUla' and 'the people of the region' without naming any people or tradition, even though the card draws on Hegra and its heritage.", "artQueries: 'Hegra rock face' will return Nabataean tomb facades, which the card then has to tell designers not to trace.", "culturalNotes: 'the contemporary desert modern lens' is vague and gives nothing to build."], "fix": "In the Credit line, name the Nabataeans who carved Hegra and the present-day communities of AlUla, and keep the card on natural strata only. Replace the Hegra query with 'AlUla rock strata' or 'sandstone cliff bands', and cut the 'desert modern lens' phrase."},
  {"id": "anodized-aluminum", "tags": ["generic", "incoherent"], "problems": ["palette and type: a gray body, one orange accent for the key control, Inter Tight with Inter, and a 'clicks' motion near 150 ms almost duplicate the ulm-school-1955 card. Swap the names and the two cards read the same.", "surprise: it sets a rule of 'one colored part per screen', but the palette carries two dyed accents (orange and cobalt), so the rule and the palette disagree.", "texture: bead blast noise and a chamfer line do not show the thing that makes anodizing unique, which is color that sits inside the metal."], "fix": "Build the card on the dyed-metal sheen: a satin color gradient of 2 to 3 percent that shifts slightly with light, on the one colored control. Choose a lead hue that is not Ulm's orange, such as cobalt or violet, and state when the second dye may appear. Use a different display face from the Ulm card."},
  {"id": "bamboo-slats", "tags": ["incoherent"], "problems": ["texture: 'vertical ribs at 8 px pitch' conflict with the idea and motion. The idea describes horizontal list rows like the slats of a blind, and the motion has the blind roll up slat by slat."], "fix": "Make the slats horizontal: 8 px rows with node marks staggered along them, on the header band and drawer only, so the texture, the list rhythm and the roll up motion all follow one axis."}
]}
```

```json
{"flags": [
{"id": "mashrabiya-screens", "tags": ["region", "culture-name", "incoherent"], "problems": ["region: marked gulf, but culturalNotes and artQueries center on Cairo, which is mena, so the card mixes an Egyptian form with the Gulf.", "culturalNotes: folds Hejazi rawasheen into mashrabiya, while avoid tells the designer to keep clear of rawashin, so the card contradicts itself.", "culturalNotes: 'Arab woodworkers' and 'across the Arab world' are loose and name no place or people."], "fix": "Make it Cairene mashrabiya with region mena, crediting the turners (kharrat) of Cairo, and drop the rawasheen line. Alternatively, rewrite it as Hejazi rawashin of Jeddah's historic district with region gulf and credit Hejazi carpenters."},
